# OpenAI Codex CLI (https://github.com/openai/codex, `npm install -g
# @openai/codex`). Verified against codex-cli 0.147.0 by actually running
# it through this switchboard's own startup-watch logic, not guessed:
#
#   - Bare `codex` launches straight into the interactive TUI in the
#     current directory — no name/session-label argument like Claude Code's
#     `--remote-control [name]`. Codex's own positional [PROMPT] argument
#     is an initial chat message, not a label, so unlike claude.engine this
#     CMD deliberately does NOT use {name} — passing the project name there
#     would feed it to the agent as if you'd typed it as your first prompt.
#   - First run in any given directory shows a one-time "Do you trust the
#     contents of this directory?" prompt — functionally identical to
#     Claude Code's "trust this folder" prompt, and, like Claude's, it's
#     remembered per-directory afterwards (recorded in
#     ~/.codex/config.toml under [projects."<path>"]) so this only fires
#     once per project.
#   - Signing in (ChatGPT login / device code / API key) is a separate,
#     one-time, account-level step — same category as Claude Code's own
#     login, and just as deliberately left OUT of STARTUP_MATCH/SEND below:
#     that's a credential decision for a human, not something to script.
#     Run `codex` once yourself as RUN_USER first to get that out of the
#     way, same as you would for `claude`.
#   - Codex does have its own `codex remote-control` subcommand, but it's
#     explicitly labeled [experimental] and is a raw websocket/unix-socket
#     protocol for a *custom* TUI client to connect to — not a hosted
#     browser link the way Claude Code's `claude.ai/code/session_...` URL
#     is. There's nothing to point URL_REGEX at, so no URL_REGEX is set
#     here — same as aider.engine, this runs via the switchboard's
#     built-in ttyd fallback instead, which needs no cooperation from
#     Codex at all and gives you a real interactive terminal (Codex's own
#     per-command approval prompts, if any, work in it exactly like they
#     would over SSH).
LABEL=Codex
CMD=codex
STARTUP_MATCH_1=Do you trust the contents of this directory
STARTUP_SEND_1=1

# HEADLESS_*: one bounded, non-interactive turn (backlog item 6a,
# docs/spec.md), used by app/teams.py's agent_run() -- unrelated to the
# interactive session above, never read by instance_start(). Partially
# verified by actually running it (docs/implementation.md): `codex exec
# {resume} --json --skip-git-repo-check "<prompt>"` really does start,
# stream NDJSON (`thread.started` carries `thread_id`, the session id used
# for {resume}; `turn.started`/`turn.failed`/`error` all confirmed live),
# and exit with a real nonzero code -- but the account codex was run under
# in that environment was not logged in (401 from api.openai.com), so a
# *successful* (exit 0) turn, `item.completed` message events, and the
# `resume <SESSION_ID>` subcommand-swap syntax specifically are NOT yet
# confirmed end to end -- see docs/implementation.md's verification table
# before trusting this beyond "the plumbing runs a real process".
HEADLESS_CMD=codex exec {resume} {schema} --json --skip-git-repo-check
HEADLESS_FORMAT=codex-jsonl
HEADLESS_PROMPT=arg
HEADLESS_RESUME=resume {session_id}

# HEADLESS_SCHEMA_FLAG (backlog item 6c, docs/spec.md "Engine-file
# extension"): presence of this key is what makes _lead_tier_for_engine()
# auto-detect this engine as tier 2 (schema-constrained lead output) rather
# than tier 3 (prose-parse). {schema_file} (NOT {schema} -- see
# docs/spec.md "Correction: {schema} is inline for Claude, a file for
# Codex", found by actually running the real claude CLI during this
# sub-spec's build) is substituted with the path of a schema.json file
# agent_run(..., schema=...) writes under the run's own throwaway rundir.
# `codex exec --help` documents `--output-schema <FILE>` as "Path to a JSON
# Schema file describing the model's final response shape" -- a real file
# path, unlike Claude Code's own --json-schema, which wants the schema's
# JSON text inline (see claude.engine's own HEADLESS_SCHEMA_FLAG). See
# docs/implementation.md for this sub-spec's own verification status
# (codex.engine's headless plumbing was already only partially verified as
# of 6a -- see the HEADLESS_CMD note above; codex remains unauthenticated
# in the environment 6c was built in, so a real tier-2 run against it is
# also not yet confirmed end to end).
HEADLESS_SCHEMA_FLAG=--output-schema {schema_file}
